  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe serves 4 and takes about 30 minutes from start to finish."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gyudon"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is a Japanese beef bowl that has fed city workers and students for over a century. It is a"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "donburi"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dish, meaning everything goes on top of a bowl of steamed rice. The beef simmers in a sweet soy and"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dashi"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "broth that soaks into the rice underneath."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Buy beef sliced as thinly as possible. Japanese supermarkets sell shabu-shabu beef, but any butcher can cut sirloin to about 2mm. If you slice it yourself, freeze the joint for 30 minutes first so it firms up and cuts cleanly. Do not overcook the beef. It only needs 2 to 3 minutes once it hits the pan or it will toughen."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 4,
          "items": [
            {
