      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the rice according to the packet. Keep it warm while you make the beef."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the dashi, soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar into a wide frying pan. Bring to a simmer over a medium heat and stir until the sugar dissolves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the grated ginger to the pan. Stir it in and let the broth bubble gently for 2 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the beef slices in a single layer. They will cook quickly. Turn them after 1 minute. Cook for another 1 to 2 minutes until just cooked through. Do not stir vigorously or the slices will clump."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the warm rice between 4 bowls. Spoon the beef and plenty of broth over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter over the spring onions and sesame seeds. Serve straight away."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The beef is tough and chewy",
              "cause": "Overcooked or sliced too thick",
              "fix": "Slice the beef no thicker than 2mm and cook for no more than 2 to 3 minutes in the simmering broth."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The broth tastes flat",
              "cause": "Dashi was weak or not enough soy",
              "fix": "Add another tablespoon of soy sauce and a teaspoon of mirin. Taste and adjust before serving."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The rice is waterlogged from the broth",
              "cause": "Too much liquid ladled over",
              "fix": "Use a slotted spoon to lift the beef first, then add broth gradually rather than pouring the whole pan over."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gyudon was born in Tokyo in the Meiji era. Cheap beef from Western-influenced butcheries met the Japanese rice bowl tradition. Chain restaurants like Yoshinoya have served it around the clock since the 1890s. It is the fast food of Japan in the best possible sense. Quick to make, filling, and deeply satisfying on a cold evening."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}